.TH DEX\-EXHIBIT\-APPLY 1 "August 19, 2026" "dexd 0.1.0" "dexd manual"
.SH NAME
dex\-exhibit\-apply \- write the exhibit config's forced display mode into the
kernel command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sudo dex\-exhibit\-apply
.RB [ \-\-exhibit\-config
.IR path ]
.RB [ \-\-cmdline\-path
.IR path ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
A forced display mode is a
.B video=
option on the kernel command line, in
.IR /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt .
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply
reads
.BR kms_force ,
.B connector
and
.B display_mode
from the exhibit config and writes that option there. Run it as root after
every edit to
.B kms_force
or
.BR connector .
.PP
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply
rewrites only the
.B video=
option naming the configured connector. Every other option keeps its value and
its position, and so does every other connector's
.B video=
option. A
.B kms_force
of
.B none
removes the option for that connector instead of writing one. A second run over
an already matching file changes nothing and reports
.BR "no change" .
.PP
Before any change it copies the current line to a timestamped backup beside it.
It writes the new line to a temporary file and renames that over
.IR cmdline.txt ,
so a power cut during the write cannot leave a Raspberry Pi that will not boot.
.PP
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply
prints
.B REBOOT REQUIRED
only when the file changed. dexd binds the display from the running kernel's
.IR /proc/cmdline ,
so the new option takes effect at the next boot.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-exhibit\-config " path"
The exhibit config to read. Default: whichever of
.I /opt/dex/exhibit.yaml
and
.I /opt/dex/exhibit.json
exists. At most one may exist.
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply
refuses when both exist and names both files: keep exactly one, or name the
file with
.BR \-\-exhibit\-config .
The file extension selects the parser:
.B .json
is strict JSON,
.B .yaml
and
.B .yml
are YAML. This is the same search
.BR dexd (1)
makes, so the boot options and dexd always read the same file.
.TP
.BI \-\-cmdline\-path " path"
The file to rewrite. Default
.IR /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt .
Test rig only: it points the rewrite at a copy, leaving the real boot options
alone.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage text and exit with status 2.
.SH CONFIG KEYS
.TP
.B asset
The video to play: a file name beside the config, such as
.BR artwork.265 ,
or an absolute path.
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply
does not use it. You may leave it out, but then nothing names a video and dexd
refuses to start rather than guess one.
.TP
.B display_mode
Required.
.BR auto ,
or
.B WxH@R
with a whole\-number rate, such as
.BR 3840x2160@30 .
It is what dexd asks mpv for.
.TP
.B kms_force
Default
.BR none .
.BR none ,
or
.B WxH@R
with an optional trailing
.BR D ,
such as
.BR 3840x2160@30D .
This is the value written into
.IR cmdline.txt .
The
.B D
makes the connector count as connected before a display is attached, for a
projector that presents its EDID late.
.TP
.B connector
Default
.BR HDMI\-A\-1 .
The HDMI port the display is plugged into, and the connector whose
.B video=
option
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply
rewrites.
.TP
.BR display ", " venue ", " note
Free text recording what this installation is. dexd type\-checks them and reads
them nowhere else.
.PP
dexd refuses an unrecognised key instead of ignoring it, so a misspelled
.B kms_forse
stops the next start rather than dropping a forced display mode. The extension
decides the format, and dexd refuses YAML in a file named
.BR .json .
Name the file for what is in it.
.SH STARTUP CHECKS
Two checks at the player's next start rest on these values. Both exit 2 rather
than start on a display mode nobody set.
.PP
The cmdline check compares
.B kms_force
with the
.B video=
option in the running kernel's
.IR /proc/cmdline .
Each refusal names both repairs, because either side may be the stale one: the
boot options may carry a leftover force, or a force this venue needs that a
newly written config omits.
.PP
The resolution pre\-flight compares the
.B WxH
half of
.B display_mode
with the modes the connector lists in
.IR /sys/class/drm/card*\-<connector>/modes ,
before the video is opened. That list has no rate column, so the
.B @R
half is checked for grammar alone. A rate the connector does not offer fails
later: mpv reports
.B "Could not find mode matching 3840x2160@60"
when the video output starts, and the player restarts instead of playing. Set
.B display_mode
to a rate the display offers, or to
.BR auto .
.SH EXAMPLES
Swapping the display on an installed player:
.PP
.EX
# set display_mode and kms_force for the new display
sudoedit /opt/dex/exhibit.yaml
sudo dex\-exhibit\-apply
.EE
.PP
The last command prints one of two lines. After
.B REBOOT REQUIRED
run
.BR "sudo reboot" .
After
.B "no change"
the edit touched no boot option, and
.B "sudo systemctl restart dexd"
picks it up at the next player start.
.PP
A display that needs the force: a 4K capture device announces 3840x2160 at
30 Hz as its preferred mode, and the graphics driver builds no 4K mode from
that announcement. Forced, the same mode works (measured; see
.IR docs/design/measurements.md ).
Set
.B display_mode
to
.B 3840x2160@30
and
.B kms_force
to
.BR 3840x2160@30D .
.PP
A display that must not be forced: a 2560x1440 monitor whose EDID never
mentions 2160 shows its preferred mode correctly. Set
.B display_mode
to
.B auto
and leave
.B kms_force
at
.BR none .
Prefer
.B auto
to naming the mode: many panels offer one fractional refresh and nothing near
it \(em one such monitor offers its 2560x1440 at 59.95 Hz alone \(em where
.B 2560x1440@60
matches no mode and the player restarts, while
.B auto
takes the connector's own preferred timing. Forcing 4K here transmits a
picture the monitor cannot show, and the screen stays black.
.SH FILES
.TP
.IR /opt/dex/exhibit.yaml ", " /opt/dex/exhibit.json
The exhibit config, next to the video it names. At most one of the two.
.TP
.I /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt
The kernel command line, one line of space\-separated options.
.TP
.I /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt.bak\-<seconds>
The backup, named for the second it was written.
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply
keeps the five most recent and names each older one it removes on standard
output.
.TP
.I /boot/firmware/cmdline.txt.new
The temporary file the new line goes to before the rename.
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply
removes it when the rename fails, and the original stays as it was.
.TP
.I /proc/cmdline
The running kernel's command line, which the cmdline check compares the exhibit
config against.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
.I cmdline.txt
now matches the exhibit config, either because
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply
rewrote it or because it already matched.
.TP
.B 1
A file could not be read or written.
.I cmdline.txt
is either untouched or fully replaced, never half\-written.
.TP
.B 2
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply
refused and wrote nothing: the command was not run as root, no exhibit config
was found or the one found is invalid, the command line was not understood, or
the rewrite would produce an empty or multi\-line
.IR cmdline.txt .
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.TP
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply: applying <path> (connector <C>, kms_force <F>)
The first line of a run that got as far as reading the config. Check this line
first when the display mode is wrong: it names the file this run read and the
two values it acts on.
.TP
.B "dex\-exhibit\-apply: <path> already matches the exhibit config \-\- no change"
Status 0, nothing written and no reboot needed. An edit that touched only
.B asset
or
.B display_mode
takes effect at the next player start:
.BR "sudo systemctl restart dexd" .
.TP
.B "REBOOT REQUIRED \-\- dexd binds the display from the running kernel's /proc/cmdline"
Status 0, after the lines naming the old line, the new line and the backup. The
forced display mode takes effect at the next boot.
.TP
.B "error: dex\-exhibit\-apply must run as root (sudo dex\-exhibit\-apply)"
Status 2, checked before any file is opened, so a run without
.BR sudo (8)
stops with every file as it was.
.TP
.B error: no exhibit config found (looked for <paths>)
Status 2. Create
.I /opt/dex/exhibit.yaml
next to the video, or name a config with
.BR \-\-exhibit\-config .
.TP
.B error: <n> exhibit configs exist at once (<paths>)
Status 2. Keep exactly one file, or name the intended one with
.BR \-\-exhibit\-config .
Switching from JSON to YAML leaves the older file in place, and the message
gives the
.BR rm (1)
line for it.
.PP
Every message dexd and its tools print, with the repair for each, is listed in
.IR docs/guides/reference.md .
.SH UPGRADES
A player set up before the display mode moved into the exhibit config may still
carry a service override file (a drop\-in under
.IR /etc/systemd/system/dexd.service.d/ )
that passes
.BR \-\-mode .
The packaged unit passes no arguments, and installing the package prints a note
when such a file is present. A drop\-in whose value agrees with the config
changes nothing until it is removed; one that disagrees makes dexd refuse to
start, naming both values. Check the directory first, because this removes every
override, not only the
.B \-\-mode
one:
.PP
.EX
ls /etc/systemd/system/dexd.service.d/
sudo rm /etc/systemd/system/dexd.service.d/*.conf
sudo systemctl daemon\-reload
.EE
.PP
dexd does not read a config left under
.I /etc/dex
either. Move it next to the video, but only if
.I /opt/dex
has no config yet, since two configs refuse:
.PP
.EX
sudo mv /etc/dex/exhibit.* /opt/dex/ && sudo rmdir /etc/dex
.EE
.PP
If
.I /opt/dex
already holds a config, copy the values across by hand and delete the old file.
.SH NOTES
Writing boot options is a separate step, run by hand.
.BR dexd (1)
runs as an unprivileged user and cannot write to the boot partition;
.B dex\-exhibit\-apply
is the privileged half, and it changes one option in one file.
.PP
Write refresh rates as whole numbers in both
.B display_mode
and
.BR kms_force .
The kernel's
.B video=
grammar has no fractional rate, and dexd refuses a fraction such as
.B @30000/1001
or a decimal such as
.B @29.97
in either key. mpv rejects the fraction while it parses its options, and rounds
.B @29.97
to the nearest whole number, so it asks for the same 30 Hz mode that
.B @30
names (measured on a Raspberry Pi 4; see
.IR docs/design/measurements.md ).
.PP
Do not edit
.I cmdline.txt
by hand. State the display mode in the exhibit config: a hand edit the config
does not match stops the player at its next start.
.PP
Where
.B kms_force
is
.BR none ,
.BR dex\-wait\-hdmi (1)
is the fallback. It runs before dexd and waits until an HDMI connector reads
.BR connected .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dexd (1),
.BR dex\-wait\-hdmi (1),
.BR systemd.service (5)
.PP
